## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement cross-topic atomicity, a critical reliability pattern in real-world streaming pipelines. It assesses correctness under partial failures and the candidate's approach to end-to-end guarantees. 

## Key Concepts
- KCNA transactions across multiple topics
- Atomic commit/abort paths and retry logic
- Transactional offsets and downstream idempotence
- Failure injection and test harness design

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo KCNA transactional producer usage
const txn = producer.initTransaction({ transactionalId: `txn-user-${userId}` });
try {
  producer.send({ topic: 'payments', key: userId, value: JSON.stringify(payment) }, txn);
  producer.send({ topic: 'ledger', key: userId, value: JSON.stringify(ledger) }, txn);
  producer.commitTransaction(txn);
} catch (e) {
  producer.abortTransaction(txn);
  throw e;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this approach across many partitions and ensure low latency?
- What monitoring and alerting would you add to detect and recover from partial commits?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design real-time access control in a multi-tenant KCNA deployment with minimal downtime and strong auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Live policy engine design
- Token lifecycle and revocation mechanisms
- Cross-region propagation and consistency
- Tamper-evident audit proofs and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-token validation sketch
function validateToken(token, tenantId, currentTime) {
  const payload = decodeAndVerify(token);
  if (!payload || payload.tenantId !== tenantId) return false;
  if (payload.exp < currentTime) return false;
  if (isRevoked(tenantId, payload.jti)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

